  "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EHeart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF) is generally defined by symptoms or signs of heart failure and a left ventricular ejection fraction of 40% or less. Guideline-directed medical therapy aims to reduce mortality and hospitalisation, relieve symptoms and improve quality of life.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EMain Medication Classes\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThe four main medication classes are:\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERenin-angiotensin system inhibitors:\u003C/strong\u003E An angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor (ARNI) is often preferred. An ACE inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker may be used if an ARNI is unsuitable. A 36-hour washout is required when switching between an ACE inhibitor and sacubitril/valsartan.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBeta blockers:\u003C/strong\u003E Evidence-based options include bisoprolol, carvedilol, controlled-release metoprolol succinate and nebivolol. These are started at a low dose in stable patients and titrated gradually.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMineralocorticoid receptor antagonists:\u003C/strong\u003E Spironolactone and eplerenone require monitoring of potassium and renal function.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESGLT2 inhibitors:\u003C/strong\u003E Dapagliflozin and empagliflozin provide benefit in people with or without diabetes and require little dose titration.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EAdditional Therapies\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003ELoop diuretics relieve congestion but should be adjusted to the lowest dose that maintains euvolaemia. Selected patients may also benefit from ivabradine, hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate, intravenous iron, or device therapy such as an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator or cardiac resynchronisation therapy.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EBarriers to Optimisation\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003ECommon barriers to optimisation include hypotension, bradycardia, renal impairment, hyperkalaemia, medication cost and limited follow-up. Clinicians should look for reversible causes before stopping therapy and consider dose reduction rather than complete withdrawal where it is safe. Early initiation, regular review and shared care with heart failure nurses, pharmacists and GPs can improve the use of these medications.\u003C/p\u003E"
}]]></Value>
    </bodyContent>
